"Chromatic Polynomial Evaluation Spectra (https://arxiv.org/abs/2512.19600)\nciteturn7academia12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 10 months ago; Title: Chromatic Polynomial Evaluation Spectra ... Around 10 years ago, Agol and Krushkal showed that the number of chromatic polynomials $P_{G}$ arising from graphs $G$ on $n$ vertices grows exponentially with $n$, by establishing that the (dual) flow polynomial $F_{G}\\left(\\frac{3+\\sqrt{5}}{2}\\right)$ already takes on exponentially many values, if one varies $G$ over all planar cubic graphs $G$ on $n$ vertices. ... The case $q=3$ confirms a conjecture of Agol, which was initially motivated by the $\\mathsf{NP}$-completeness of planar $3$-colorability.\nTitle: Chromatic Polynomial Evaluation Spectra\nAuthors: Rafael Miyazaki, Cosmin Pohoata, Michael Zheng\nDate: Mon Dec 22 17:33:40 2025\n\nAround 10 years ago, Agol and Krushkal showed that the number of chromatic polynomials $P_{G}$ arising from graphs $G$ on $n$ vertices grows exponentially with $n$, by establishing that the (dual) flow polynomial $F_{G}\\left(\\frac{3+\\sqrt{5}}{2}\\right)$ already takes on exponentially many values, if one varies $G$ over all planar cubic graphs $G$ on $n$ vertices. We show, more generally, that the size of the set $\\{P_G(q): |V(G)|=n\\}$ is exponential in $n$, for every fixed real number $q \\neq 0,1,2$. In fact, our approach can also be pushed to show that $P_{G}(q)$ already takes on exponentially many values, if we only vary $G$ over all planar graphs on $n$ vertices. The case $q=3$ confirms a conjecture of Agol, which was initially motivated by the $\\mathsf{NP}$-completeness of planar $3$-colorability.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nOn recurrence for $$\\mathbb {Z}^d$$ -Weyl systems | Monatshefte für Mathematik | Springer Nature Link (https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s00605-025-02088-9)\nciteturn7search0 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.5 years ago; Crawled: last month; Note that we avoided framing Katznelson’s question as a conjecture, as neither of the potential answers seems more likely than the other. ... For instance, every minimal system can be obtained through proximal and equicontinuous extensions from a strictly PI system (for background see [2] Chapter 14 or [5] Chapter 6), or by passing through maximal d-step nilsystems and their maximal distal factor (see [18] and [21] for background). ... For general locally compact abelian group actions, we begin in Sect. 3 defining Bohr recurrence using the theory of duality and proving that this definition is equivalent to the usual definition using equicontinuous systems. ... Katznelson’s question: Is every set of H-Bohr recurrence a set of H-recurrence?\nWithin this context, the class of nilsystems has proved to be particularly important. Nilsystems have been used, for example, in the ergodic context of multiple convergence along arithmetic progressions [20] and in the topological context to construct explicit examples of sets of multiple recurrence^{Footnote 1} [10, 23].\n\nIn our study, we focus on sets of Bohr recurrence, which are sets that exhibit recurrence for all rotations^{Footnote 2}. One is naturally led to ask if this is enough to be a set of recurrence. This is typically known as Katznelson’s question, popularized by Katznelson [24] for \\(\\mathbb {Z}\\)-actions.\n\nNote that we avoided framing Katznelson’s question as a conjecture, as neither of the potential answers seems more likely than the other. For a negative answer, there are some potential counterexamples that are known to be good for recurrence in circle rotations, but it remains unclear whether they are good for recurrence across all systems [9, 11, 17]. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nOn subsets of integers having dense orbits - ScienceDirect (https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0001870826001258)\nciteturn7search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: 6 months ago; Crawled: 2 months ago; The Katznelson's question asks if any Bohr recurrence sequence is necessarily a Birkhoff one.\nA central open problem in the field connecting dynamics, combinatorial number theory, and group theory is the Katznelson's question. To frame it, we need to introduce some definitions.\n\nWe say that $A\\subset \\mathbb{N}$ is a Birkhoff (resp. Bohr) recurrence sequence, denoted by $A\\in {\\mathcal{F}}_{Bir}$ (resp. $A\\in {\\mathcal{F}}_{Bohr}$), if for any minimal (resp. minimal equicontinuous) system $(Y,S)$, there is $y\\in Y$ such that ${S}^{{n}_{i}}y\\to y$ for some ${\\{{n}_{i}\\}}_{i=1}^{\\infty }\\subset A$. The Katznelson's question asks if any Bohr recurrence sequence is necessarily a Birkhoff one.\n\nThis problem remains unresolved and has attracted significant attention due to its deep connections with diverse mathematical areas. For related results and recent advances, we refer to [40], [39], [33], [29], [21], [12], [25] and the references therein. Particularly, Katznelson [40] obtained a number-theoretical formulation of the question and related it with graph theory; Host, Kra and Maass [29] established that for a minimal pro-nilsystem the two types of recurrence sequences coincide, and if A is a Birkhoff recurrence sequence for a minimal system $(Y,S)$, then A is also a Birkhoff one for any minimal system $({Y}^{\\prime },S)$ which is a proximal extension of $(Y,S)$; Glasscock, Koutsogiannis and Richter [21] demonstrated the equivalence of these sequences for specific skew product systems. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nThe return times theorem, auto-correlation and sequences with an empty Fourier–Bohr spectrumAll (https://www.impan.pl/pl/wydawnictwa/czasopisma-i-serie-wydawnicze/studia-mathematica/all/288/2/116288/the-return-times-theorem-auto-correlation-and-sequences-with-an-empty-fourier-bohr-spectrum)\nciteturn7search2 [wordlim: 200] Published: 6 months ago; Crawled: 2 weeks ago; ## The return times theorem, auto-correlation and sequences with an empty Fourier–Bohr spectrum\n\n### Tom 288 / 2026 ... Katznelson and D.\n\n## The return times theorem, auto-correlation and sequences with an empty Fourier–Bohr spectrum\n\n### Tom 288 / 2026\n\nMatan Tal Studia Mathematica 288 (2026), 135-154 MSC: Primary 37A46; Secondary 60G10 DOI: 10.4064/sm250117-8-1 Opublikowany online: 6 May 2026\n\n#### Streszczenie\n\nThis paper explores the proof by J. Bourgain, H. Furstenberg, Y. Katznelson and D. S. Ornstein (1989) of their return times theorem and lights a corner in it regarding the role of auto-correlation. As for pointwise convergence, this was already observed by E. Lesigne, C. Mauduit and B. Mosse (1994), and here we write down the proof. This yields a more intrinsic characterization of the sequences satisfying the pointwise theorem. Then we obtain a characterization linked to auto-correlation also for sequences satisfying the mean theorem – by that theorem those were already known to be exactly the sequences with an empty Fourier–Bohr spectrum. Some examples are provided regarding generic sequences satisfying the pointwise theorem for which the measure on the circle that the auto-correlation function represents (by Fourier transform) is not atomless, and also regarding the existence of sequences that satisfy the mean theorem but not the pointwise one.\n\n#### Autorzy\n\n  * Matan Tal Einstein Institute of Mathematics\nEdmond J. Safra Campus\nThe Hebrew University of Jerusalem\nJerusalem, Israel\ne-mail--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nERGODIC THEORY: RECURRENCE (https://citeseerx.ist.psu.edu/document?doi=d0d5b5ec713772c8564547563b4aa2bcdaf8895f&repid=rep1&type=pdf)\nciteturn7search13 [wordlim: 200] Published: 8 months ago; Theorem 6.3 (Furstenberg & Katznelson [38]). ... A set S ⊂N is a set of Bohr recurrence if for every\nsome related results about vector spaces over ﬁnite ﬁelds ([37]). Again, the\nonly known proof for the density version of the Hales-Jewett theorem relies\nheavily on ergodic theory.\n7. Open Problems\nIn this section we formulate a few open problems relating to the material\nin the previous three sections. It should be noted that this selection reﬂects\nthe authors’ interests, and does not strive for completeness.\nWe start with an intriguing question of Katznelson ([53]) about sets of\ntopological recurrence. A set S ⊂N is a set of Bohr recurrence if for every\nα1, . . . , αk ∈R and ε > 0 there exists s ∈S such that {sαi} ∈[0, ε]∪[1−ε, 1)\nfor i = 1, . . . , k.\nProblem 1. Is every set of Bohr recurrence a set of topological recurrence?\nBackground for this problem and evidence for a positive answer can be\nfound in [53] and [71]. As we mentioned in Section 4, there exists a set\nof topological recurrence (and hence Bohr recurrence) that is not a set of\nrecurrence.\nProblem 2. Is the set S = {l!2m3n : l, m, n ∈N} a set of recurrence? Is it\na set of k-recurrence for every k ∈N?\nIt can be shown that S is a set of Bohr recurrence. Theorem 4.3 cannot\nbe applied since the uniform distribution condition fails for some irrational\n13A combinatorial line in Wn(A) is a set consisting of k words with n letters, that\nhave ﬁxed letters in l positions, for some 0 ≤l < n, and the remaining n −l positions\nare occupied by a variable letter x, for x = a1, . . . , ak. For example, in W4(A) the sets\n{(a1, x, a2, x): x ∈A} and {(x, x, x, x), : x ∈A} are combinatorial lines.\n14For example, the line {(a1, x, a2, x): x ∈A} corresponds to the progression m, m +\nn, m + 2n, m + 3n where m = a1 + a2d2 and n = d + d3.\n16--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nChromatic Polynomial Evaluation Spectra | alphaXiv (https://www.alphaxiv.org/abs/2512.19600)\nciteturn7search3 [wordlim: 200] Published: 10 months ago; Crawled: 2 months ago; The case $q=3$q=3 confirms a conjecture of Agol, which was initially motivated by the $\\mathsf{NP}$NP-completeness of planar $3$3-colorability. ... @misc{miyazaki2025chromaticpolynomialevaluationspectra, title={Chromatic Polynomial Evaluation Spectra}, author={Rafael Miyazaki and Cosmin Pohoata and Michael Zheng}, year={2025}, eprint={2512.19600}, archivePrefix={arXiv}, primaryClass={math.CO}, url={https://arxiv.org/abs/2512.19600}, }\nSubmitted 22 Dec 2025\n\n# Chromatic Polynomial Evaluation Spectra\n\nRM\n\nRafael Miyazaki\n\nCP\n\nCosmin Pohoata\n\nMZ\n\nMichael Zheng\n\n## Abstract\n\nAround 10 years ago, Agol and Krushkal showed that the number of chromatic polynomials $P_{G}$P G​ arising from graphs $G$G on $n$n vertices grows exponentially with $n$n, by establishing that the (dual) flow polynomial $F_{G}\\left(\\frac{3+\\sqrt{5}}{2}\\right)$F G​(2 3+5​​) already takes on exponentially many values, if one varies $G$G over all planar cubic graphs $G$G on $n$n vertices. We show, more generally, that the size of the set $\\{P_G(q): |V(G)|=n\\}${P G​(q):∣V(G)∣=n} is exponential in $n$n, for every fixed real number $q \\neq 0,1,2$q=0,1,2. In fact, our approach can also be pushed to show that $P_{G}(q)$P G​(q) already takes on exponentially many values, if we only vary $G$G over all planar graphs on $n$n vertices. The case $q=3$q=3 confirms a conjecture of Agol, which was initially motivated by the $\\mathsf{NP}$NP-completeness of planar $3$3-colorability.\n\nView Paper\n\n\nv1\n\n## Citation\n\nCopy\n\n@misc{miyazaki2025chromaticpolynomialevaluationspectra, title={Chromatic Polynomial Evaluation Spectra}, author={Rafael Miyazaki and Cosmin Pohoata and Michael Zheng}, year={2025}, eprint={2512.19600}, archivePrefix={arXiv}, primaryClass={math.CO}, url={https://arxiv.org/abs/2512.19600}, }\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nERGODIC THEORY: RECURRENCE (https://citeseerx.ist.psu.edu/document?doi=2515ae57f401245595bb5bf216fb5c8b26ac5184&repid=rep1&type=pdf)\nciteturn7search14 [word